'My Project'

## Git-backed distributed ledger

This repository includes a small append-only JSONL ledger with a SHA-256 hash
chain. It uses only the Python standard library.

```powershell
python .\ledger.py add "Example: Alice pays Bob 10"
python .\ledger.py verify
git add .\ledger.py .\ledger.jsonl
git commit -m "Record ledger entry"
git push
```

To get another copy, clone or pull this repository. Before adding a new entry,
pull the latest changes, then verify the ledger. Concurrent edits may conflict
and need to be reconciled before committing.

The hash chain detects accidental or unsynchronized edits; it is not consensus,
identity verification, or a guarantee against someone rewriting and recommitting
the entire history. Do not use this toy example as a production financial ledger.

## Simple Excel-friendly ledger

Open `ledger.csv` in Excel and add one transaction per row. The columns are
date, recorder, description, income, expense, and balance. For a group, everyone
uses the same file and puts their name from the roster in the recorder column.
CSV is plain text, so Git can show changes more clearly and merge them more
easily than an `.xlsx` workbook.

Before editing, pull the latest version; after editing, review the diff and
commit the change. Concurrent edits can conflict. Git records which configured
account made a commit, but neither that nor the recorder column verifies a
person's identity. This is a shared, versioned ledger, not a blockchain with
consensus or protection from someone changing the file and committing it.
